<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Byzantine–Bulgarian war of 894–896</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/894">894</a>–<a href="/years/896">896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Byzantine–Bulgarian war of 894–896 was fought between the Bulgarian Empire under Simeon I and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> of Leo VI, after the Bulgarian market in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>. Byzantium enlisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>, who ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, but Simeon allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> against them and defeated the Byzantines at Boulgarophygon in 896, forcing them to pay an annual tribute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byzantine-bulgarian-war-of-894-896.jpg" alt="Byzantine–Bulgarian war of 894–896" width="320" height="133" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: from the Middle Ages, unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boulgarofygon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Under Boris I (r. 852–889) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> converted to Eastern Orthodox Christianity, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-preslav">Council of Preslav</a> in 893 Old Bulgarian replaced Greek as the language of the church, the Byzantine clergy were expelled and Boris's third son Simeon was made prince. These decisions ended Byzantine hopes of influence over the newly Christian country.</p><p>In 894 Stylianos Zaoutzes, the leading minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-vi-the-wise">Leo VI the Wise</a>, persuaded the emperor to move the Bulgarian market. Under the Treaty of 716 and later agreements, Bulgarian merchants had lived in their own colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and paid favourable taxes; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> they paid higher tariffs and had to buy eastern goods through middlemen close to Zaoutzes. Byzantine chroniclers held that Simeon sought a pretext to seize the Byzantine throne, but historians such as Vasil Zlatarski and John Fine regard his attack as a defence of Bulgarian trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the autumn of 894 Simeon invaded Byzantine Thrace and defeated an army under Prokopios Krenites and Kourtikios, probably near Adrianople; he had the noses of captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> guardsmen cut off and sent them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Leo VI then sent Nicetas Scleros to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a> and Kurszán. In 895 the Byzantine navy under Eustathios Argyros broke an iron chain across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and ferried the Magyars south. Led by Árpád's son Liüntika, they defeated Simeon, who took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silistra">Drastar</a>, and pillaged as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a>.</p><p>Simeon proposed a truce and kept the envoy Leo Choirosphaktes waiting with letters and new demands while he allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>. Early in 896 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-bug">Southern Bug</a>, reportedly losing 20,000 riders themselves, and the Pechenegs prevented the Magyars from returning to their homeland, so that they moved west into the Pannonian Basin. In the summer Simeon invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> again and routed the Byzantine army under Leo Katakalon at Boulgarophygon, where the protovestiarios Theodosius fell.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The peace restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>'s status as most favoured nation, lifted the commercial restrictions and ceded the area between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strandzha">Strandzha</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> released the Byzantine soldiers and civilians they held, allegedly 120,000. The treaty lasted formally until Leo VI's death in 912, though Simeon broke it after the Sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 904 to gain land in Macedonia. He began a building programme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> to rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and imposed his authority on the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> by recognising Petar Gojniković as its ruler.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Byzantine–Bulgarian war of 894–896” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
